19. MODEL INTEGRITY VALIDATION
Before regulatory validation begins, the engine must verify model integrity.
Initial checks include:
orphaned objects
invalid parent references
missing levels
duplicate identifiers
invalid geometry references
missing required relationships
invalid object states
broken dependency references
If model integrity fails at a blocking level, downstream validation may be prevented from executing.

20. GEOMETRIC VALIDATION
The engine consumes geometry generated by the Geometry Engine.
It must verify:
closed boundaries
non-self-intersection
valid solids
valid profiles
object containment
object intersection
minimum separation
clearances
alignment
elevation consistency
Geometry validation does not recreate the geometry engine.
It evaluates the geometry engine's authoritative outputs.

21. SPATIAL VALIDATION
Spatial rules evaluate relationships such as:
room area
room dimensions
circulation width
clearance
door swing
fixture clearance
occupancy density
travel distance
vertical circulation
The engine obtains required thresholds from the applicable regulatory manifest or project requirements.

22. ACCESSIBILITY VALIDATION
Accessibility validation shall evaluate applicable requirements including:
accessible routes
door clear widths
maneuvering clearances
corridor widths
accessible parking
washroom clearances
vertical accessibility
threshold conditions
turning spaces
accessible fixtures
The actual requirements are sourced from the project's applicable jurisdictional rule set.

23. LIFE-SAFETY VALIDATION
The engine shall support computational evaluation of:
occupant load
egress capacity
exit count
exit separation
travel distance
common path
dead-end conditions
exit discharge
fire separations
fire-resistance requirements
protected shafts
stairs
ramps
The engine reports findings; it does not represent a professional approval.

24. CODE VALIDATION
Code validation uses:
CODE_MANIFEST
+
CODE_RULES
+
PROJECT_MODEL
The evaluator resolves:
applicability
threshold
exception
condition
measurement
result
The result must preserve the originating rule citation.

25. CODE TRACEABILITY
Every code finding must be traceable to:
code_source_id
code_rule_id
code_manifest_id
rule_version
retrieval_timestamp
This prevents a generic AI statement from being treated as a regulatory determination without provenance.

26. REQUIREMENT VALIDATION
Project requirements are validated independently of code requirements.
Example:
client requirement:
minimum 3 bedrooms
The engine may evaluate:
actual bedrooms = 2
and return:
FAIL
even if the project is otherwise code-compliant.

27. DESIGN INTENT VALIDATION
Design intent may be represented through structured project requirements.
Examples:
preserve existing tree
maintain exterior envelope
maintain south-facing glazing
retain existing stair
minimum parking count
Aria may translate natural-language intent into structured requirements, but validation executes against the structured representation.

